{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To solve the problem of seating 7 people around a round table such that no two of the 3 specific people (Pierre, Rosa, and Thomas) sit next to each other, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the total number of unrestricted seating arrangements:**\n   Since the table is round, we fix one person in one position to avoid counting rotations as distinct. Let's fix Pierre in one position. The remaining 6 people can be arranged in \\(6!\\) (6 factorial) ways.\n   \\[\n   6! = 720\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the number of arrangements where at least two of Pierre, Rosa, and Thomas sit next to each other:**\n   We will use the principle of inclusion-exclusion to count these arrangements.\n\n   - **Case 1: Exactly two of Pierre, Rosa, and Thomas sit next to each other.**\n     Treat the two people who sit next to each other as a single \"block\". This \"block\" and the remaining 4 people (including the third person from Pierre, Rosa, and Thomas) make 5 \"blocks\" to arrange around the table. Fixing one of these blocks (Pierre), we have 4! ways to arrange the remaining blocks. Within the \"block\", the two people can switch places, so there are 2 ways to arrange them.\n     \\[\n     4! \\times 2 = 24 \\times 2 = 48\n     \\]\n     There are 3 ways to choose which two of Pierre, Rosa, and Thomas form the \"block\", so the total for this case is:\n     \\[\n     3 \\times 48 = 144\n     \\]\n\n","prefix_end":1372,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":true,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,11625,279,3491,315,37299,220,22,1251,2163,264,4778,1965,1741,429,902,1378,315,279,220,18,3151,1251,320,47,44423,11,46830,11,323,11108,8,2444,1790,311,1817,1008,11,582,686,1795,1493,7354,1447,16,13,3070,47866,279,2790,1372,315,77839,37299,27804,25,1019,256,8704,279,1965,374,4778,11,582,5046,825,1697,304,825,2309,311,5648,25009,69201,438,12460,13,6771,594,5046,36977,304