,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,641,1246,1657,5510,646,400,22,3,1251,2444,2163,264,4778,1965,421,902,1378,315,279,400,18,3,1251,36977,11,46830,11,323,11108,646,2444,1790,311,1817,1008,30,320,1514,1095,27804,892,525,69201,315,1817,1008,525,11758,438,279,1852,6138,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nIn how many ways can $7$ people sit around a round table if no two of the $3$ people Pierre, Rosa, and Thomas can sit next to each other? (Seating arrangements which are rotations of each other are treated as the same.)","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"6ba371631a2e79ca9f7a3480d26155f9f979bf41eae65c0437cabae773d79fc8"},"seconds":15.562875270843506,"seed":637982330216227503,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem of seating 7 people around a round table such that no two of the 3 specific people (Pierre, Rosa, and Thomas) sit next to each other, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the total number of unrestricted seating arrangements:**\n   Since the table is round, we fix one person in one position to avoid counting rotations as distinct. Let's fix Pierre in one position. The remaining 6 people can be arranged in \\(6!\\) (6 factorial) ways.\n   \\[\n   6! = 720\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the number of arrangements where at least two of Pierre, Rosa, and Thomas sit next to each other:**\n   We will use the principle of inclusion-exclusion to count these arrangements.\n\n   - **Case 1: Exactly two of Pierre, Rosa, and Thomas sit next to each other.**\n     Treat the two people who sit next to each other as a single \"block\". This \"block\" and the remaining 4 people (including the third person from Pierre, Rosa, and Thomas) make 5 \"blocks\" to arrange around the table. Fixing one of these blocks (Pierre), we have 4! ways to arrange the remaining blocks. Within the \"block\", the two people can switch places, so there are 2 ways to arrange them.\n     \\[\n     4! \\times 2 = 24 \\times 2 = 48\n     \\]\n     There are 3 ways to choose which two of Pierre, Rosa, and Thomas form the \"block\", so the total for this case is:\n     \\[\n     3 \\times 48 = 144\n     \\]\n\n   - **Case 2: All three of Pierre, Rosa, and Thomas sit next to each other.**\n     Treat the three people as a single \"block\". This \"block\" and the remaining 4 people make 5 \"blocks\" to arrange around the table. Fixing one of these blocks (Pierre), we have 4! ways to arrange the remaining blocks. Within the \"block\", the three people can be arranged in 3! ways.\n     \\[\n     4! \\times 3! = 24 \\times 6 = 144\n     \\]\n\n   Using the principle of inclusion-exclusion, the number of arrangements where at least two of Pierre, Rosa, and Thomas sit next to each other is:\n   \\[\n   144 + 144 = 288\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the number of valid arrangements:**\n   Subtract the number of invalid arrangements from the total number of unrestricted arrangements.\n   \\[\n   720 - 288 = 432\n   \\]\n\nThus, the number of ways to seat 7 people around a round table such that no two of Pierre, Rosa, and Thomas sit next to each other is \\(\\boxed{432}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{432}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"counting_and_probability:262"}
